  "worked_solution": "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,\u21d2\\,{c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =2{c}_{1}x+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=5$ and ${c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 5 & =2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1-{c}_{1}}{2} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{3{c}_{1}+1}{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =3\\,\u21d2\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=3{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}.\n$$",
